ISSUE: DND's lying around all over the place.

The DND doesn't really hold any information that's important. You can keep
track of the same stuff elsewhere.  I would like to replace DND's with a
cache of DND FCB's, and hook them together via drive/starting-cluster
pairs.  A file is identified on disk by the drive/clust pair for the
directory it's in, the clust/offs of its FCB in that directory, and its own
starting cluster number.

ISSUE: FTAB/OFD is an incomplete implementation of file handles

Currently: FTAB holds a pointer to an OFD, an owner ID, and a reference
count. 

The OFD contains a reference count and the read/write pointer information,
plus the file info like time, date, start cluster, and size, and DMD and
DND and such.

The correct way is to have FTAB entries contain an owner and a pointer  to
an intermediate structure, holding the RW pointer info; that in turn has a
reference count (how many FTAB's point to it) and a pointer to the (single)
OFD for that file.  If the file is opened multiple times, there's a new OFD
for each open.  If the handle is dup'd, a new FTAB entry is created
pointing to the same RW pointer structure.

ISSUE: a process' current-directory spec (however it's done) can be
rendered invalid because a drive gets media changed or the directory gets
deleted. If you create a new directory in that same place (same drv/clust
or same struct pointer), then that will become the current directory of the
process!  Hmm. The current system works: it has a slot in the diruse/dirtbl
table for each current directory that's used, and it resets the slot to
"root" when you get media-change.  This will not be "freed" until the
process terminates.

An alternative is to have a chain of all basepages and fix up each one.
This is bad because of people using Pexec for nefarious purposes.  The
alternative is to keep the current system (dammit!) where the basepage
contains an index into a table.  This can be bad if inheritance is done
wrong.  Hell, screw 'em!  Have to handle the case of a drive/clust pair
which aren't valid any more...

ISSUE: "hidden directory" is not valid under GEMDOS.  It is under MSDOS,
and therefore can exist on some disks.  Why not allow directories to have
attributes like that?  But do you fail to find a hidden  directory if the
search attribute is 0x20?  In any case, such directories shouldn't be
treated as files.  In a related development, what about reserved attribute
bits?  Probably shouldn't return "access denied" in these cases; what if we
decide we want to do something with them?

